#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087360 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd63cb30-a236-546e-82ac-08bce09c88f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "8ab1df94-0c4f-5720-a9b6-939dba0ab9c6" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1219" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=4^{x}-1$." :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=4^{x}{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/46dd42e2c001e441acef975d236e8368.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y=4^x-1$, we take the graph of $y=4^x$ and shift it $1$ unit down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0)$, and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/75376b1492dfea9adebd588fbff21737.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087361 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "65c814f8-df2f-549d-9467-c6dda6890bb3" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1220" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y=3^{x}+c$. What is the value of $c$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/c96ce14cb7e8d6abd52346f6c446bc92.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y=3^x$ is $y=0$. The vertical translation\n\n$$\ny=3^x+c\n$$\n\nshifts the asymptote to $y=c$.\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2$. Therefore, $c=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087362 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f73207f4-4661-55f1-acfb-cc5fe6ba9316" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1221" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3$." :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=3^{x}$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/a9e5c91e185135efce8960d4a81e3626.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)$. To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $5$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5)$, but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/db1ae2508e49de5eaaedad1694025119.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3$. To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,5)$ to $(0,2)$, and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/4da5afb2a94cb83ada4a74de870a1159.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087363 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "62f7d6e1-61ec-5f3d-8cdd-4da0c90c7ccf" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1222" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=k(6^{x})+c$. Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/817/Source/Images/9dbe1a3f16086ee26e329717d5be9e2a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve\n\n$$\ny=k(6^{x})+c\n$$\n\nis $y=c$. In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4$, so $c=4$.\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k(6^{x})+4\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y$. The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6)$, so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= k(6^{x}) + 4 \\\\\n6 &= k(6^{0}) + 4 \\\\\n6 &= k(1) + 4 \\\\\n6 &= k + 4 \\\\\n2 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is\n\n$$\ny=2(6^{x})+4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895794fe-c257-5933-b561-b3993c250b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085329} {:answer/id #uuid "c279ef11-cf95-515a-9293-05231b09e412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085330} {:answer/id #uuid "de9ab1fa-f979-5cb8-90d3-6e2516192156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085331} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc144a1-f709-55b3-9f07-9b6d074a0f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085332} {:answer/id #uuid "56a5658c-31cb-55b9-8e3e-4da9072c1fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de9ab1fa-f979-5cb8-90d3-6e2516192156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085331} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f2be1a2-87ef-5ec5-a1d8-5411e8273458" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085328}] :question/id #uuid "a9e76cd0-6e63-52b6-ad92-c0e43ec54487" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-30270" :question/problem "For the curve $y = 5(6^{x}) + 2$, find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis."}]}
